        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="0562" n="514" sameAs="frus:frus1868p1/pg514"/>

                <head>Mr. Williams to Mr.
                        Seward</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No<hi rendition="#italic">.</hi> 13.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1868-06-08"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >June</hi> 8, 1868.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Referring to my dispatch No. 3, of January
                    11, 1868, relating to the reduction of the duty on tea-dust, I have now the
                    honor to send you an additional correspondence supplementary to that,
                    (inclosures A, B.) It appears that a question was started as to what was
                    properly included under the term <hi rendition="#italic">tea-dust,</hi> and in
                    order to obviate any further doubt the Chinese government has reduced the limit
                    under which the half duty is to be levied, from that costing 15 taels per pecul
                    to that costing 10 taels. However, as no tea-dust has been in market for many
                    years whose cost has reached 10 taels, the change has no practical bearing. It
                    is said it has been customary at some ports to admit tea-dust from Japan at an
                    ad valorem duty of five per cent., but the import is trifling. At Tientsin 765½
                    peculs of tea-dust and tea stalks entered last year, valued at 3,033 taels, or
                    four taels per pecul.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S. WELLS WILLIAMS.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>




